a rapidly growing financial trend is  attempting to turn traditional banking  on its head it's called decentralized  finance or defy and it promises to bring  digital banking for cryptocurrencies to  the masses but with no middlemen and  almost no barriers to entry so in the  last 18 months this idea has really  mushroomed within the crypto market  estimates vary but a conservative one  says the total value locked in d5  reached 114 billion dollars in november  this year and while that's just a  fraction compared with traditional  banking d5 is growing quickly it has  increased more than 1200 percent since  june 2020.  here's why some are calling d5 the wild  west of finance where investors are  entering uncharted territory to take on  big risks for the chance of even bigger  rewards  when we talk about defy we're not  talking about a specific financial  product or service  defy is just the next iteration of this  this grand experiment in  cryptocurrencies which is basically just  to to recreate the financial system in a  digital online format paul vina has been  reporting on the equities market for  almost 25 years it has been covering  cryptocurrencies since 2013. anything  you could do at a bank or a brokerage is  handled rather than through an  individual and a corporation it's  handled through a computer program  you can think of the emergence of defy  like this  a dusty town in the wild american west  where there's no sheriff no bank and no  cash  here at the local trading post people  can trade and lend goods and do pretty  much anything else normally done at a  bank it isn't owned by a single person  or entity and unlike traditional finance  trades that happen here are verified out  in the open where everyone can see for  example one type of transaction that  takes place at the trading post is  lending to insure the trade the borrower  gives collateral for the loan the lender  then earns interest off this deal in the  same way a bank would  transactions like this are all recorded  on a public ledger that everyone can see  in the world of defy this ledger is  called a blockchain which is a network  of computers built to store and record  transactions  every computer on that network has an  identical copy of that ledger so that  all of the data is open all of it is  public this is different from a  traditional stock trade which works like  this  i have a broker the other party has a  broker the company has an institution  that keeps track of the stock itself all  these different middlemen all these  different intermediaries have their own  ledgers have their own internal  databases where they're recording the  transactions a blockchain takes care of  all of that and does it in an open way  that anybody can track the progress of  that trade  most defy services run on ethereum's  technology which also hosts the  cryptocurrency ether it's one of the  most commonly traded cryptos in d5 along  with stable coins digital assets which  are pegged to currencies like the dollar  the d5 services run on the blockchain  similar to how apps run on your phone  and the mechanism that allows them to  work is called a smart contract  smart contract is an idea i've been  around for years it's another app that  is focused on a bank-like service so all  it's really doing is taking all the  parameters of a trade and sticking them  into fields and automating it and then  monitoring it smart contracts are  designed to give defy transactions more  transparency and security and because  all the parameters are being inputted  into a program ostensibly it's more  secure because there there shouldn't be  any real debate or contradiction about  the terms of the service  despite this layer of security  decentralized finance carries a lot of  risk  in many cases the people facilitating d5  transactions are anonymous this has  caused fraud to become rampant  a poorly created smart contract could  have loopholes that allow scammers to  steal or other design flaws that affect  the value of assets  another risk is security and because  most defy services aren't insured if a  platform fails or is hacked millions of  dollars can be on the line the problem  is that  you may not know who wrote the program  you may not know how secure the program  itself is  that's a significant significant risk  that you really can't overlook  these d5 protocols  virtually once a week there is a problem  with one of them  so far this year investors have lost  about one and a half billion dollars to  defy attacks hacks and cons and in this  wild west of finance regulation is still  virtually non-existent though that could  change  here's the chairman of the securities  and exchange commission speaking with  the wall street journal about d5 but if  this field is going to have an ability  to move forward  it's it's going to be inside some public  policy framework  and inside some sort of regulatory  perimeter  look folks have already been hurt in  this field while decentralized finance  is risky many think the potential  benefits are worth it benefits are an  ability to make money  you are either lending out your  cryptocurrencies and you're getting  interest paid back on that and that  interest can be anywhere from five to  twenty percent even more you can be a  passive investor and earn money on an  asset that you were probably storing in  a wallet that was doing nothing the  other benefit is you can borrow against  your holdings a lot of these services  are being used as a way for people to  get a bigger pool of cryptocurrency to  go through bets out into the derivatives  market so it's a way for you to turbo  charge your cryptocurrency gambling  habit essentially  if defy continues growing it's likely  that regulations will be imposed to  smooth out its rough edges and reduce  risk for investors  which makes sense even in the wildest of  wild west towns a sheriff eventually  showed up to lay down the law  [Music]
 

